<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Committee of Safety (Hawaii)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Committee of Safety was a group of 13 mostly American and European businessmen and lawyers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> who organised the overthrow of the Hawaiian Kingdom in January 1893. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorrin-a-thurston">Lorrin A. Thurston</a>, it acted after Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> tried to issue a new constitution restoring royal power, and with the support of US marines it proclaimed the Provisional Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanford-b-dole">Sanford B. Dole</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/committee-of-safety-hawaii.jpg" alt="Committee of Safety (Hawaii)" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hawaiian_League_(PP-36-3-005).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The committee grew out of the leadership of the Missionary Party, whose members ran as Independent Party candidates. A Committee of Nine, formed on 14 January 1883, led the Independent ticket at the elections of 1884, at which the party won 13 seats; reconvened as the Committee of Thirteen for 1886, it fell to ten. In January 1887 the Committee of Thirteen founded the Hawaiian League, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> illegal under the kingdom's law, whose 405 members, all of European or American descent, swore to protect the white community against arbitrary action by the government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorrin-a-thurston">Lorrin A. Thurston</a> drafted its constitution.</p><p>Many members wanted annexation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> rather than reform of the monarchy, and the League was also called the Annexation Club. By early 1887 its members controlled the Honolulu Rifles, about 200 armed non-Hawaiian men under Volney V. Ashford, and in June 1887 they used them to force King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a>, which limited his power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the Marshal of the Kingdom, Charles B. Wilson, learned of the planned coup on 16 January 1893, his requests for warrants to arrest the committee were refused, and he and Samuel Nowlein gathered 496 men to protect the queen. On 17 January a policeman was shot while trying to stop a wagon of weapons, and the Rifles occupied Ali'iolani Hale. At the committee's request the American minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-l-stevens">John L. Stevens</a> had landed 162 sailors and marines from the USS Boston; they fired no shot but intimidated the royalists, and the queen ordered her forces to surrender to avoid loss of life.</p><p>The committee's proclamation, read to a crowd before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a> on 17 January, abolished the monarchy and established a Provisional Government until terms of union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> were agreed. Every government with a diplomatic presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> recognised it within 48 hours, though Heihachiro Togo, commanding the Japanese gunboat Naniwa, at first refused to strike the kingdom's colours. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grover-cleveland">Grover Cleveland</a>'s administration blocked annexation, which Congress approved only in 1898, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Committee of Safety (Hawaii)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A <b>Committee of Safety</b> led by Lorrin A. Thurston, made up mostly of American sugar interests, seized government buildings on 17 January, while the US minister John L. Stevens landed marines from USS Boston.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-hawaii">Provisional Government of Hawaii</a></li></ul></section>
